/-!
# Integrality over ℚ[j] of a Γ₀(ℓ)-invariant q-expansion

Theorem statement: `ModularCurve.isIntegral_adjoin_jq_of_hasSum_of_gamma0_invariant`
(`76cce1be-41db-50c0-ba9c-e56fef8597bb`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `ad5d288c-5260-57bd-b1bc-e4e7197deb98`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $\ell$ be a prime, let $f,g$ be formal Laurent series over $\mathbb Q$ (elements of
`LaurentSeries ℚ`), and let $F\colon\mathfrak H\to\mathbb C$ be a function on the upper half-plane.
Assume: (hF) for every $\tau\in\mathfrak H$ the family $m\mapsto f_m\,q_1(\tau)^m$, indexed by
$m\in\mathbb Z$, where $f_m\in\mathbb Q\subseteq\mathbb C$ is the $m$-th coefficient of $f$ and
$q_h(\tau)=$ `Function.Periodic.qParam h τ` $=e^{2\pi i\tau/h}$, is summable with sum $F(\tau)$;
(hG) for every $\tau\in\mathfrak H$ the family $m\mapsto g_m\,q_\ell(\tau)^m$ is summable with sum
$F(S\tau)$, where $S\in\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb Z)$ is `ModularGroup.S` acting by Möbius
transformation; and (hinv) $F(\gamma\tau)=F(\tau)$ for every $\gamma$ in `CongruenceSubgroup.Gamma0
ℓ` and every $\tau\in\mathfrak H$. The conclusion is that $f$ is integral, in the sense of
`IsIntegral`, over the $\mathbb Q$-subalgebra `Algebra.adjoin ℚ {ModularCurve.jq}` of $\mathbb
Q((q))$ generated by [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157), the Laurent series
$q^{-1}\cdot\jmath$ with $\jmath$ the image in $\mathbb Q[[q]]$ of the integral power series `jNum`;
that is, $f$ satisfies a monic polynomial equation with coefficients polynomial in the $q$-expansion
of $j$.

This is the integrality half of the $q$-expansion principle for $X_0(\ell)$: a Laurent series that
is the $q$-expansion at $\infty$ of a $\Gamma_0(\ell)$-invariant function, with a meromorphic
expansion of width $\ell$ at the cusp $0$, is integral over $\mathbb Q[j]$. It is used to place the
$q$-expansions of modular units, and their inverses, in the function field of $X_0(\ell)$ over
$\mathbb Q$.

